<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mushika dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mushakas, Mooshika dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mushika dynasty ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, in the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezhimala">Ezhimala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Kannur</a> north of the Chera kingdom, in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Its history is known from inscriptions and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mushikavamsa">Mushikavamsa</a>, an 11th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poem on its kings. The later Kolathiri rulers of Kolathunadu claimed descent from it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Mushika or Mushaka Parvata is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> rendering of the Tamil Ezhimalai, the hill of Ezhimala; mispronounced as Elimala, it came to be read as the Mountain of the Rats. Early Tamil poems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam literature</a> describe Ezhimalai on the north-western edge of the Tamil country and its rulers, a chiefly lineage called the Muvan, as enemies of the early Chera rulers who also made marriage alliances with the Chera, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> families. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> lists the Mushikas among the kingdoms of the far south.</p><p>The poems celebrate rulers named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nannan">Nannan</a>. One, active around 180 CE, was known for his gold ornaments and gilded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>; his warriors under Njimili killed the Satiyaputra ruler Neduman Anji at the battle of Pazhi, and he was finally defeated and killed by the Chera ruler Narmudi Cheral, who cut down Nannan's sacred Vaka tree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the polity became a monarchy known as Kolla-desham, along the Kavvai, Kuppam and Valappattanam rivers, and came under Chera influence, probably in the reign of Vijayaraga in the late 9th century. Its rulers supplied troops to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>. In 1005 the Senur inscription of the Chola emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a> records the defeat of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a>, Kolladesam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodungallur">Kodungallur</a>, through what seem to have been naval raids for plunder, and the Eramam inscription of about 1020 attests Chola officers in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>.</p><p>The Mushaka Vamsa Kavya, composed by the poet Athula at the court of King Srikantha, traces the line to a king consecrated by Parashurama. It tells of a king restored with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> help, of one who killed a Pallava royal and married his daughter, and of marriages and wars with the Chera king Jayaraga. After the Chera state declined, the kingdom lived on as Kolathunad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Both the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> and the medieval kingdom rested chiefly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, cardamom and other hill produce rather than on wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming. Ezhimala Hill was the first landfall on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a> for ships crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, and the port of Naravu is often identified with the Naura of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a>. Medieval ports such as Madayi, Valapattanam and Dharmapattanam hosted the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> anjuvannam, manigramam, valanchiyar and nanadeshikal, and probably Jewish traders. Unlike other Chera subordinates, the ruler commanded his own guard of companions called the Thousand.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mushika dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In later centuries the region was ruled by the <b>Mushika dynasty</b>, whose land lay north of the Chera kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/ezhimala-kingdom">Ezhimala kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Kolathiri rulers claimed descent from the earlier <b>Mushika dynasty</b> of Ezhimala and controlled the coast around Cannanore and the pepper-growing hinterland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kolathunadu">Kolathunadu</a></li></ul></section>
